{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass after these things, and it was told, etc.</b> The reason for this narrative, and also the reason for its saying <b>after these things</b> — for it has no connection to the aforementioned birth from the side of what preceded — is to inform us that Rivqah, the designated mate of Yitzhaq, was born. And the reason that it was until now and not earlier, we have already anticipated: for he was from the side of the masculine and had no feminine counterpart until after the Binding (Akeidah), when the soul of the masculine became impregnated within him — after these things it was told to Avraham, etc., <b>and Betuel begot Rivqah</b>, etc.; this is the essence of the narrative. And the reason the verse needed to write the entire sequence of those born to Nahor, also from his concubine Reumah, for no apparent purpose — know this: when the soul of a wondrous tzaddik shines forth in this world, or the righteousness of one such as Rivqah, mother of all the seed of holiness, because of what preceded — namely the admixture from evil aspects caused by the sin of the first man, in whom good and evil became attached through his iniquities — great souls from the side of the kelippah will be found in her vicinity, and when she stands, they stand with her, and when she goes out into the world, they all go out along with her in her vicinity. And each one recognized its own aspect: Rivqah kindled her light in holiness, while the others were like chaff that is tossed about. Therefore the verse informs us of all the nations that issued from Nahor, for among them was a precious pearl, Rivqah our mother, peace be upon her." "<b>Thus concludes the perasha of VaYera</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:47.155449942Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}